    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Pete Crow-Armstrong** stands as the clear anchor of the Zurich Zebras lineup with an elite score of 80.21. His high ceiling is driven by a favorable matchup against Pallante and, most critically, the presence of a pitcher with a slow delivery, which provides a significant boost to his base projection given our team\u2019s emphasis on stolen bases. **Caleb Durbin** also projects as a high-floor play at 67.73; his favorable park factors and middle-of-the-order positioning against Mason Barnett make him a reliable cornerstone for our offensive production. While **Lars Nootbaar** remains a strong start at 62.26, his value is buoyed primarily by the platoon advantage against Assad, providing a reliable floor for our outfield depth.\n\nThe most difficult roster decision today involves the bottom of the order, specifically evaluating the risk profile of **Carson Benge** (45.21). Despite a respectable platoon advantage, **Carson Benge** is firmly in the marginal tier and carries a 35% rain risk, which makes him the most vulnerable starter if late-breaking lineup news shifts our depth chart. Conversely, we are navigating a quiet day for our core stars like **Yordan Alvarez**, **Trea Turner**, **Kyle Schwarber**, and **Christian Yelich**, all of whom are sidelined due to scheduled off-days. This leaves us relying on the utility of **Junior Caminero** (57.51) to bridge the gap; while Winn\u2019s elite skill level slightly suppresses his projection, **Junior Caminero** remains a necessary start over deeper bench options like **Hyeseong Kim**, **Kazuma Okamoto**, or **Colt Keith**.\n\nWe are monitoring the status of **Jacob Gonzalez** (51.30) and **Chase Meidroth** (47.84) closely, as both are currently hovering in the marginal scoring zone. **Jacob Gonzalez** is highly dependent on his assumed #9 spot in the order, and any shift in the official lineup could quickly drop him below our required efficiency floor. Furthermore, **Chase Meidroth** faces a tough test against Weathers, and while the dynamic platoon advantage offers some insulation, his performance is the most susceptible to the volatility of the matchup. We remain cautious with lower-tier depth like **Nelson Velazquez**, **Gary Sanchez**, **Josh Smith**, **Dillon Dingler**, **Casey Schmitt**, **Pedro Ramirez**, and **Luis Pena**, keeping them in reserve unless a late scratch forces a defensive substitution.",
